.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_CREATE 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_create \- create a ccol_event_loop with default memory management
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_event_loop_create(max_events_per_wait, num_lock_stripes,
                  num_reactor_threads, err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro is a short form of ccol_event_loop_create_with_mprocs()
with mmgmt_procs = NULL, which selects the standard malloc and free.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
max_events_per_wait  Size of the epoll_wait batch buffer that the
                      poller thread uses. It must be between 1 and
                      INT_MAX, both ends included, and small enough
                      that max_events_per_wait *
                      sizeof(struct epoll_event) does not overflow
                      size_t. It bounds how many ready events one
                      epoll_wait call drains, not how many
                      registrations the loop can hold.
num_lock_stripes     Number of independent lock stripes for the
                      registry of the fds and the entries. It must be
                      >= 1; a value of 1 means a single shared lock.
                      Pass a larger value to lower the contention
                      between ccol_event_loop_add,
                      ccol_event_loop_remove and
                      ccol_event_loop_modify. That contention grows when
                      threads use many different fds and registrations
                      at the same time.
num_reactor_threads  Total number of OS threads that this loop uses
                      for its own poll work and dispatch work. It must
                      be >= 1. Whatever this value is, exactly one
                      dedicated thread ever calls epoll_wait on the
                      shared epoll instance. A value of 1 means that
                      the same thread also dispatches every callback
                      inline, while a larger value adds
                      (num_reactor_threads - 1) separate dispatch
                      worker threads.
err_str               Optional pointer that gets an error string on a
                      failure. Pass NULL to ignore it.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new ccol_event_loop handle on success.
Returns CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID on a failure, which has one of these
causes: an argument is invalid; an allocation failed;
epoll_create1, eventfd or pthread_create failed; the process had no
thread-specific key left for the dispatch of a loop; the process-exit
destructors of the library have run and the last loop is gone; or the
library could not allocate a handle slot. See
ccol_event_loop_create_with_mprocs(3).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Every background reactor thread of the ccol_event_loop that this macro
returns is running by the time that the call returns. Destroy the
loop with ccol_event_loop_destroy() after you finish with it.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_event_loop_create_with_mprocs (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_destroy (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_add (3)
